  "question": {
    "text": "In $\\Delta A B C$, if $a^{2} \\cos ^{2} A-b^{2}-c^{2}=0,$ then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Z48t7",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{4}    < A < \\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "nmbuB",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{2}    < A < \\pi$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "460I8",
      "text": "$A=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "tO7RB",
      "text": "$A    < \\frac{\\pi}{4}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "We have, $a^{2} \\cos ^{2} A-b^{2}-c^{2}=0$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad a^{2} \\cos ^{2} A=b^{2}+c^{2}$\r\nAlso in $\\triangle A B C$, we have $\\begin{aligned} \\cos A=\\frac{b^{2}+c^{2}-a^{2}}{2 b c} &=\\frac{a^{2} \\cos ^{2} A-a^{2}}{2 b c} \\\\=& \\frac{-a^{2}\\left(1-\\cos ^{2} A\\right)}{2 b c}=\\frac{-a^{2} \\sin ^{2} A}{2 b c}    < 0 \\\\ & \\quad \\because 0 < A < \\pi, a, b, c>0 \\end{aligned}$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\cos A < 0 \\Rightarrow A$ lies in IInd quadrant.\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{\\pi}{2} < A < \\pi$",
    "image": null
  },
